## SECTION 1 — HEADER BANNER
<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Tomatoes Grow Quickly.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Follow stories and descriptions about everyday experiences.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow simple stories and descriptions about planting and watering (e.g., 'I water the plants every day.').<br>Can participate in discussions about school garden tasks, answering simple questions based on oral information (e.g., 'What do they grow in the garden?' 'They grow tomatoes.' 'How often do they water the tomatoes?' 'Every day.').<br>Can get the gist of a conversation on caring for plants (e.g., 'What time do they water the plants?' 'In the morning.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of gardening items: a watering can, a plant, seeds, soil, and a tomato. Students repeat the names of the items after the teacher. The teacher asks, "What do we do in a garden?" to elicit simple responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces key vocabulary (garden, plant, tomato, seed, soil, watering can, grow, water) by showing flashcards and clearly pronouncing each word. The teacher models simple sentences like "This is a plant. It needs water. The tomato grows big." Emphasize /t/ in 'tomatoes' and /g/ in 'grow'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding using simple questions. For instance, holding up a tomato picture: "Is this a flower or a tomato?" (Expected answer: "A tomato.") Showing a watering can: "Do we drink from this?" (Expected answer: "No.") "What do we use it for?" (Expected answer: "To water plants.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short audio story/description (read by teacher or pre-recorded) about a child's garden routine. The text will incorporate target vocabulary (garden, plant, tomato, water, grow, every day, in the morning) and present simple + 's'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Audio Script:</i> "Hello! My name is Lily. I have a small garden at home. Every morning, I water my plants. My favorite plant is the tomato plant. These tomatoes are green now, but they grow quickly! My brother, Tom, also helps. He digs the soil sometimes. We like to care for our garden every day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding simple descriptions and instructions about everyday experiences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions (Listen for general understanding):</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is Lily's story about?" (Expected: Her garden/plants.)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grows quickly?" (Expected: Tomatoes.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Listen for specific details):</b> The teacher says sentences, and students show "True" (thumbs up) or "False" (thumbs down).
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Lily waters her plants in the afternoon." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Her brother helps her dig the soil." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice listening for specific details, target vocabulary (garden, plant, tomato, seed, soil, watering can, grow, water, every day, in the morning), present simple + 's', and demonstratives (this/these).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Listen and Match/Number.</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher gives students a worksheet with pictures of a seed, a small plant, and a ripe tomato (unlabeled). The teacher reads a short sequence of sentences about how a tomato grows (e.g., "First, you plant the seed. Then, the plant grows. Finally, it gives us a red tomato."). Students listen and number the pictures in the correct order (1-seed, 2-plant, 3-tomato).</p>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Listen and Complete.</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher reads sentences with missing words related to gardening, and students complete them by listening. The teacher provides two options orally for each blank. Example: "Maria ___ (waters/plants) the plants every day." Students listen and circle the correct verb on their worksheet. Focus on present simple + 's' and adverbs of frequency.</p>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My mom ____ (grow/grows) big tomatoes."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"We use a ____ (hose/seed) to water."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check: Listen and Verify.</b>
                        <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In pairs, students get a simple drawing of a garden (Partner A) and a list of items to check (Partner B). Partner A describes their garden orally (e.g., "In my garden, there is a big tomato plant. These are green tomatoes. I water it every day."). Partner B listens and checks off the items they hear on their list. Then they switch roles.</p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Listen and Draw My Garden."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Each student receives a blank sheet of paper. The teacher describes a simple garden scene using target vocabulary, grammar (present simple, demonstratives), and pragmatic functions (describing steps/routines). Students listen carefully and draw what they hear. The teacher repeats the description twice at a moderate pace.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Instructions:</i> "Listen and draw. In the middle of your paper, draw a big plant. This plant is a tomato plant. Draw three green tomatoes on the plant. On the right side, draw a watering can. On the left side, draw a small seed in the soil. Remember, we water the plants every day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Students' ability to accurately follow multiple-step oral instructions and identify key elements from a spoken description, demonstrating comprehension of vocabulary and simple sentence structures related to gardening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on accurate listening comprehension from the "Listen and Draw" activity and responses to comprehension questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students listen to a final short audio description of a daily gardening routine. They then answer 2-3 multiple-choice or short-answer comprehension questions on a simple exit slip.
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Audio:</i> "My name is Carlos. I help my grandmother in her garden. She grows flowers and vegetables. Every morning, she waters the plants with a hose. Sometimes, I help her dig the soil. We love our garden!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Questions:</i></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What does Carlos's grandmother grow? (a. trees and grass b. flowers and vegetables c. fruit and bread)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How often does she water the plants? (a. every afternoon b. sometimes c. every morning)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate identification of key vocabulary from oral input.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct comprehension of present simple routines (e.g., 'She waters').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to follow simple instructions and answer questions based on oral information.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two statements:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I understood (new words/sentences about the garden)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can follow instructions like (drawing a plant/watering)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me to understand when listening today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about the garden did I learn today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., "The flashcards and realia were highly effective for introducing vocabulary. The 'Listen and Draw' activity engaged most students and provided good insight into their listening comprehension.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for next lesson? (e.g., "Some students struggled with present simple + 's' when identifying routines. I need to reinforce this structure with more examples in future listening inputs. The audio speed might need slight adjustment for slower processors.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How will this lesson connect to the next lesson? (e.g., "In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will encounter a short text about a garden, using the same core vocabulary and simple sentence structures introduced today. They will read about the routines we discussed and practiced listening for.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>